1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,437
A coronary angiogram answers a question:
is there a narrowing or blockage in an

2
00:00:05,442 --> 00:00:07,982
artery that supplies the heart muscle?

3
00:00:08,356 --> 00:00:12,867
In this example, the cardiologist reaches
those arteries through the patient’s right

4
00:00:12,872 --> 00:00:18,068
groin. We’ll follow the instruments to the
heart, see how contrast creates the

5
00:00:18,073 --> 00:00:21,705
images, and return to the groin for
recovery.

6
00:00:22,172 --> 00:00:26,198
The heart muscle gets its blood supply
from the coronary arteries.

7
00:00:26,572 --> 00:00:31,307
Plaque can build up in an artery wall and
make the channel narrower.

8
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:36,078
An angiogram may be planned after a review
of symptoms and other tests.

9
00:00:36,173 --> 00:00:41,196
In some suspected heart attacks, the team
may need an urgent angiogram and may go on

10
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:42,708
to treat the artery.

11
00:00:43,174 --> 00:00:47,268
For a planned angiogram, your team
explains the test and reviews your

12
00:00:47,273 --> 00:00:51,717
medicines, kidney health and any previous
reaction to contrast.

13
00:00:52,091 --> 00:00:55,874
Follow their instructions about food,
drink and medicines.

14
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:00,866
In the cath lab, staff monitor your heart
rhythm and blood pressure.

15
00:01:01,077 --> 00:01:02,700
You are usually awake.

16
00:01:02,714 --> 00:01:06,079
The team can give medicine to help you
relax if needed.

17
00:01:06,453 --> 00:01:11,779
The common femoral artery runs beneath the
skin in the upper thigh near the groin.

18
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:15,529
Here the team uses ultrasound to identify
it.

19
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:19,883
The skin is cleaned and the area is
covered with a sterile drape.

20
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:24,678
Local anaesthetic numbs the spot where the
artery will be entered.

21
00:01:25,261 --> 00:01:30,492
After numbing the site, the cardiologist
uses a fine access needle to enter the

22
00:01:30,497 --> 00:01:33,630
common femoral artery under ultrasound.

23
00:01:34,004 --> 00:01:37,403
The needle tip is shown inside the artery.

24
00:01:37,684 --> 00:01:41,502
A fine access wire passes through the
hollow needle.

25
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:47,202
X-ray helps the team confirm that the wire
follows the main iliac artery.

26
00:01:47,669 --> 00:01:50,732
The needle slides back out over the wire.

27
00:01:50,943 --> 00:01:55,550
Watch how the wire stays in the artery and
preserves the entry path.

28
00:01:55,924 --> 00:02:01,155
The team passes a temporary micropuncture
introducer with its inner dilator over the

29
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:06,068
fine wire. The fine wire and inner dilator
are taken out.

30
00:02:06,442 --> 00:02:11,769
The outer introducer stays in the artery
so the team can exchange wires.

31
00:02:12,143 --> 00:02:17,736
A larger exchange wire goes through the
temporary introducer and into the artery.

32
00:02:18,203 --> 00:02:22,218
The temporary introducer comes out over
the exchange wire.

33
00:02:22,592 --> 00:02:25,039
The exchange wire stays in place.

34
00:02:25,412 --> 00:02:30,669
The working femoral sheath and its dilator
slide in over the exchange wire.

35
00:02:31,043 --> 00:02:34,512
The team removes the exchange wire and the
dilator.

36
00:02:34,793 --> 00:02:37,380
The working sheath stays at the groin.

37
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:41,583
The team can take a separate groin image
to check the entry site.

38
00:02:41,956 --> 00:02:45,728
A longer diagnostic guidewire passes
through the sheath.

39
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:52,531
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, lets the
cardiologist see where the wire is going.

40
00:02:52,812 --> 00:02:56,931
The diagnostic catheter advances over the
long guidewire.

41
00:02:57,398 --> 00:03:00,879
The short sheath remains where the team
entered the artery.

42
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:06,437
The wire and catheter travel through the
right external and common iliac arteries,

43
00:03:06,442 --> 00:03:09,633
then up the abdominal and chest aorta.

44
00:03:10,099 --> 00:03:14,994
They round the aortic arch toward the
aortic root, where the coronary arteries

45
00:03:14,999 --> 00:03:17,829
begin above the aortic valve.

46
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:22,820
At the aortic root, the long guidewire is
withdrawn from the catheter.

47
00:03:23,031 --> 00:03:28,749
Here the curved catheter tip is positioned
at the left coronary opening, above the

48
00:03:28,754 --> 00:03:30,145
aortic valve.

49
00:03:30,519 --> 00:03:35,590
The cardiologist injects X-ray contrast
through the diagnostic catheter.

50
00:03:35,964 --> 00:03:39,991
For a moment, it outlines the channel that
blood travels through.

51
00:03:40,364 --> 00:03:45,363
Watch the left main artery fill and then
see two of its main branches come into

52
00:03:45,368 --> 00:03:49,557
view. Now look at the same moment on the
X-ray.

53
00:03:49,931 --> 00:03:53,818
The dark branching lines show where the
contrast is flowing.

54
00:03:54,285 --> 00:03:59,577
In this groin example, the team uses a
different catheter for the right artery.

55
00:03:59,951 --> 00:04:04,848
The left catheter moves back from the
coronary opening and is withdrawn.

56
00:04:05,314 --> 00:04:08,018
The working sheath stays at the groin.

57
00:04:08,113 --> 00:04:12,743
The right diagnostic catheter follows a
guidewire up through the aorta.

58
00:04:12,791 --> 00:04:17,721
The team withdraws the wire and positions
the catheter tip at the right coronary

59
00:04:17,726 --> 00:04:23,375
opening. The cardiologist injects contrast
separately at the right coronary

60
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:29,531
opening. The right coronary artery and its
branches briefly appear on X-ray.

61
00:04:29,998 --> 00:04:33,847
The imaging arm can move around the
patient to record the arteries from

62
00:04:33,852 --> 00:04:35,232
different angles.

63
00:04:35,605 --> 00:04:40,665
On this example image, the contrast-filled
channel is thinner at one spot.

64
00:04:41,039 --> 00:04:46,293
Another angle helps the cardiologist check
where the narrowing is and how severe it

65
00:04:46,298 --> 00:04:52,330
looks. Sometimes a pressure measurement or
imaging from inside the artery helps

66
00:04:52,335 --> 00:04:55,363
answer what the angiogram alone cannot.

67
00:04:55,829 --> 00:05:00,689
If no major blockage is visible and
symptoms persist, the team may look for

68
00:05:00,694 --> 00:05:05,995
other causes. The next step depends on the
findings, the symptoms, and whether the

69
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:07,832
situation is urgent.

70
00:05:08,206 --> 00:05:13,182
It may be medicines, another test,
angioplasty, or a discussion about bypass

71
00:05:13,187 --> 00:05:18,452
surgery. Angioplasty uses a balloon and
may place a stent to treat a

72
00:05:18,457 --> 00:05:23,320
narrowing. In selected cases the team can
do that in the same session.

73
00:05:23,473 --> 00:05:26,234
Here, the test ends after imaging.

74
00:05:26,248 --> 00:05:30,411
After the images are complete, the
cardiologist withdraws the diagnostic

75
00:05:30,416 --> 00:05:32,306
catheter through the sheath.

76
00:05:32,517 --> 00:05:34,881
The sheath is still at the access site.

77
00:05:34,886 --> 00:05:39,711
For manual closure, the team removes the
groin sheath and presses firmly over the

78
00:05:39,716 --> 00:05:42,198
entry site to control bleeding.

79
00:05:42,665 --> 00:05:48,258
In selected cases, the team uses a closure
device, such as a plug or stitch system.

80
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:52,577
Staff check the site and leg circulation
before the patient gets up.

81
00:05:52,951 --> 00:05:57,662
Staff check your pulse, blood pressure,
groin and leg circulation.

82
00:05:57,943 --> 00:06:02,306
You may need to lie flat with the leg
straight while the site is monitored.

83
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:08,076
After an uncomplicated planned test, many
patients can go home the same day.

84
00:06:08,357 --> 00:06:13,414
Ask what the angiogram showed, when you
can walk, lift or drive, and whom to

85
00:06:13,419 --> 00:06:15,275
contact with questions.

86
00:06:15,858 --> 00:06:20,334
Follow the written instructions for the
dressing, activity, medicines and

87
00:06:20,339 --> 00:06:24,992
follow-up. A small bruise or mild soreness
can happen.

88
00:06:25,273 --> 00:06:30,516
Contact the care team promptly for
increasing redness or pain, drainage or

89
00:06:30,521 --> 00:06:31,540
fever.

90
00:06:32,123 --> 00:06:36,913
Call local emergency services for heavy
bleeding or bleeding that continues

91
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:42,706
despite firm pressure, rapidly growing
swelling, a newly cold or numb foot, new

92
00:06:42,711 --> 00:06:45,832
chest pressure or sudden shortness of
breath.

93
00:06:46,415 --> 00:06:49,094
The groin gives the team a route to the
heart.

94
00:06:49,468 --> 00:06:54,862
The catheter delivers contrast, the X-rays
show the coronary arteries, and the images

95
00:06:54,867 --> 00:06:56,954
help guide the next decision.

96
00:06:57,328 --> 00:07:01,503
Before you go home, make sure you know
your result and what to do during

97
00:07:01,508 --> 00:07:02,724
recovery.
